      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"
