      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}=(- 4 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2}\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- 4 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 24 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 24 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(24 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{9}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=24 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=9.$"
